SUMMARY:After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics
DESCRIPTION:After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics                                                                           \n        \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n	\n\n	\n\n\n			\nEvents\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    \n\nOctober 9\, 2026 – April 25\, 2027\nExhibition Hall. Third Floor\nIncluded with admission. Free for Gardiner Members\n\n\n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n  Get Tickets\n\n\n\n  Become a Member\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Clay receives the gestures of the human body. Hands press\, scrape\, squeeze\, and pour it. Forms are fixed by heat\, becoming permanent yet breakable. \nAfter the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics brings together four Canadian artists with roots in the Caribbean—PJ Anderson\, Eddy Firmin\, Manuel Mathieu\, and Sharon Norwood—who use clay to explore what our bodies inherit\, protect\, and carry forward. Spanning anglophone and francophone perspectives and different journeys of migration\, their work challenges the idea of the island as isolated and self-contained\, tracing connections across the region’s histories and geographies. \nStep inside a scented shelter where fragrance triggers memory. Take the controls of an interactive video game and navigate a life-sized digital rendering of the artist’s body. Discover historic bricks made by enslaved Africans whose finger and hand markings are still visible on their surfaces. Across sculpture\, painting\, and immersive installation\, the exhibition reveals how expansively contemporary artists are thinking through clay. \nAfter the Island is a collaboration between the Gardiner Museum\, Black Artists’ Networks in Dialogue (BAND)\, and the Museum of the African Diaspora (MoAD)\, resulting in two exhibitions presented concurrently at the Gardiner in Toronto and MoAD in San Francisco. Modelling a novel curatorial partnership\, this work explores new modes of thinking and co-creation. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    NOTICE: Scented Exhibition \nSome artworks in this exhibition include scent. If you are sensitive to odors\, please be advised. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n                      \n                Programs\n            \n            \n                        \n            \n                                     \n                    \n                                                \n                    \n                                            \n              \n                                            \n        \n        \n        \n        \n                        \n                        \n        \n               \n        \n                    \n                \n                        \n                \n            \n                                    \n                After the Island: Public Opening\n                \n            \n                         \n                Oct 8 2026 @ 7:30 pm\n                                                    \n                Join the artists and curators for evening of art\, music\, and community. Free with registration!\n            \n                        \n                        \n                Learn more\n            \n                    \n                \n                                \n        \n               \n        \n                    \n                \n                        \n                \n            \n                                    \n                After the Island: Artist Panel\n                \n            \n                         \n                Oct 9 2026 @ 6:00 pm\n                                                    \n                Curators Joséphine Denis and Dr. Sequoia Miller will moderate a panel featuring artists PJ Anderson\, Eddy Firmin\, Manuel Mathieu\, and Sharon Norwood\, followed by a Q&A.\n            \n                        \n                        \n                Learn more\n            \n                    \n                \n                                \n    \n                    \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Exhibition Partners\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Presenting Sponsors\n                \n                                \n                            \n                    \n                Supporting Partners\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Government Support\n                \n                                \n                                \n                            \n                    \n                Project Partner\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Media Partners\n                \n                                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Thanks To\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Image: Eddy Firmin\, Sans signe\, 2023\, Ceramic\, wood\, fabric\, straw\, Variable dimensions. Photo: Michael Patten \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/after-the-island-caribbean-canadian-ceramics/
