SUMMARY:Adaptive Ceramic Histories
DESCRIPTION:Adaptive Ceramic Histories                                                                          \n        \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n	\n\n	\n\n\n			\nEvents\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    \n\nNovember 20\, 2026 – March 29\, 2027\nThe Hilary and Galen Weston Foundation Hall. Ground Floor\nIncluded with admission. Free for Gardiner Members.\n\n\n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n  Get Tickets\n\n\n\n  Become a Member\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Curated by Alexandra Box\, Gardiner Museum Curatorial Resident \nEngage in an exploration of materially mediated dynamics of care and adaption. Adaptive Ceramic Histories presents an expansive way of looking at histories of medical and adaptive ceramics created for sick or disabled users. The exhibition invites you to delve into the design of care from the vantage point of users\, departing from traditional histories of medicine centering on medical professionals. Instead\, it considers the possibilities of pleasure and metaphor to build a more fulsome knowledge of patient care to craft the future. \nAdaptive Ceramic Histories features objects from the Gardiner Museum’s permanent collection alongside loans to highlight the importance of ceramic materials at different moments in the history of Western medicine from the 17th century to the present day. \nThe Gardiner Museum Curatorial Residency is made possible through the generous support of the Rebanks Family. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    About the Curator\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Alexandra Box\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Alexandra Box is an arts professional and artist. She has been awarded fellowships and artist residencies through Medalta Potteries\, Salt Spring Arts Council\, and Point Gallery. Alexandra is a curatorial participant in the Knowledge Incubator & Mentorship Initiative through Redefine Arts. Her writing has been published by Hearth\, Peripheral Review\, and The Capilano Review. Alexandra serves on the Board at Forest City Gallery and is a contributor to the Media Collaboratory. She holds a BFA (2020) in ceramics and curatorial practices from Emily Carr University and an MA (2024) in Art History from the University of Toronto. \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Header image: Feeding cup\, Rouen\, France\, early 1700s\, Tin-glazed earthenware\, The Pierre Karch and Mariel O’Neill-Karch Collection\, G14.4.20 \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/adaptive-ceramic-histories/
